# Coverage floors, enforced by scripts/coverage.sh in CI.
#
#   package <import path>  <pct>   floor for a whole package
#   file    <repo path>    <pct>   floor for one file
#
# Package floors are a ratchet set a few points below current coverage, so an
# ordinary refactor does not trip them. RAISE a floor when you raise its
# coverage — a ratchet nobody ratchets stops being a floor and becomes a
# historical note. Every package that reports coverage needs an entry; one
# without a floor fails the gate, so new code cannot land ungated.
#
# File floors are not a ratchet. They are pinned at 100 where a package floor
# cannot see the risk: a package averages its files, so internal/secrets can sit
# comfortably above 90 while a branch in the scanner that decides whether a
# credential is reported at all goes untested.
#
# The org floor is 75 (nanohype/standards/testing-rubric.json). Packages below it
# carry the measured number and the reason.

# ── security-critical: per-file 100%, no ratchet ──────────────────────────────
# The secret scanner decides whether an exposed credential is reported at all. A
# branch it never took in a test is a detection nobody has proven fires.
file    internal/secrets/patterns.go   100
file    internal/secrets/scanner.go    100
# The exit code is this tool's contract with a merge gate: 0 supports approval,
# 2 rejects, 3 says the scan could not see enough to answer either way. A wrong
# branch here reports a red run as green, which is worse than any single missed
# finding.
file    cmd/gate.go                    100
# The compliance evaluator decides whether an auditor is told a CIS or SOC 2
# control passed. An untaken branch here is a verdict nobody has proven the tool
# can reach, and a verdict is the one output a reader cannot check for
# themselves. The loaders sit at 100 for the same reason one layer down: they
# decode the reports the verdicts are computed from, and a field that silently
# fails to decode turns a domain full of findings into a control with nothing to
# evaluate.
file    internal/compliance/evaluate.go  100
file    internal/compliance/loader.go    100
# The exec-credential guard: the whole control on whether a kubeconfig NAMED BY
# THE MODEL may run an arbitrary command in a process holding live AWS
# credentials. It sits in its own file because a package floor cannot reach it —
# four lines inside cmd, which is at 17%, so it measured 0% while the package sat
# above its floor.
file    cmd/mcp_cluster.go               100

# ── packages at or above the org floor ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A floor below 75 does not belong under this heading. internal/audit and
# internal/output are listed with the section below for that reason: the heading
# is a claim about the entries under it, and two of them contradicted it.
package internal/certs              90
package internal/cloud              78    # capability interfaces, shared types and the tag policy
package internal/compliance         97
package internal/cloud/aws          82
package internal/cost               95
package internal/drift              88
package internal/fix                95    # Terraform/shell emitters and the `iam fix` entry path: the severity floor, per-principal dedup, and an unwritable file each pinned
package internal/iam                88
package internal/inventory          85
package internal/network            94
package internal/orphans            95
package internal/output/sinks       84
package internal/platform           86
package internal/providers          85
package internal/repo               82
package internal/quota              95
package internal/secrets            93
package internal/storage            90
package internal/tags               94
package internal/baseline           75    # the store and its shared path definition

# ── the total, ratcheted ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing here compared any number to the org floor: the per-package floors are
# each set below it, so every one could be met while the tree as a whole sat
# under 75. The gate printed the total and gated on nothing.
#
# This does not assert 75 — the tree does not meet it, and a floor set to a
# number the tree fails is a gate that is red on arrival and gets removed. It
# asserts the total cannot REGRESS, and names the org floor it has not reached.
total   coverage                    65    # the org floor is 75; the gate prints the measured total every run, and the toolchain's statement counting moves it

# ── packages below the org floor ──────────────────────────────────────────────
package internal/audit              73    # 74.2 measured
package internal/output             73    # 77.0 measured; floored below the org floor
# These are the repo's real coverage debt. Each carries what it measures, and
# raising one is a change to this file, not permission to stay.
package internal/compare            64    # report normalization
package internal/cloud/k8s          57    # most paths need a live apiserver; the RBAC analyzer and the connection resolver are the covered parts
package internal/report             60    # aggregation over saved reports
package cmd                         17    # handlers resolve providers and render; the logic they call is floored above
